// @reject: a dictionary is written `#(…)`, and the bare `(a: 1)` is refused.
// @reject: Until v0.0.9 the colon was the WHOLE of what told `(a: 1)` from
// @reject: `(1, 2)` — COLLECTIONS.md said so and accepted it deliberately,
// @reject: because the alternative was a notation of its own and `{}` is the
// @reject: block delimiter of the entire language.
// @reject:
// @reject: What forced the notation was the EMPTY one. `()` would have to be
// @reject: both the empty tuple and the empty dictionary, and they are not the
// @reject: same value: one takes `d["k"]$~ v` and the other answers "tuples
// @reject: are immutable". The empty dictionary was reachable — take the only
// @reject: key out of `(a: 1)` and `$#` is 0 — and could not be written, so
// @reject: every program that built one at run time started it with an
// @reject: invented key and removed it afterwards (GAP-ZYB-003).
// @reject:
// @reject: `#` is the meta/type mark, the same one `#[…]` uses to declare an
// @reject: array's mix: saying which of the two a pair of parentheses opens is
// @reject: a statement about its type. Keys may now be strings too, which the
// @reject: bracket always accepted and the literal never could (GAP-ZYB-004).
// @reject:
// @reject: Both spellings would have been worse than either: two ways to write
// @reject: one thing is what the mark was introduced to end. 276 literals were
// @reject: migrated across the corpus, the applications and the examples.
d = (a: 1, b: 2)
